course: dictionary
lesson: 5642
title: Many meanings of link
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. Link can be a noun or a verb. «There is a link between sugar and tooth decay» uses link to mean a connection between two things.
explain: «The chain had one broken link» uses link to mean one ring or loop of a chain. «Click the link to go to the next page» uses it to mean a word or picture you click on a website. Same spelling, three different meanings.
explain: As a verb, link means to connect or join. «Link arms» means join arms, and «link the two ideas» means connect the two ideas. Ask yourself: what is being joined or connected? The answer will show you the meaning.
fact: A link is a connection between two things, people or ideas.
fact: A link can also be one ring or loop of a chain.
fact: On a website, a link is a word or picture you click to go to another page.
fact: Scientists look for links between things, but a link is not always a cause.
fact: «Most words are arbitrary: the link between a word and its meaning is a habit of the speakers, not something in nature.»
exercise: «There is a link between sugar and tooth decay.» What does link mean here? | A ring in a chain | *A connection between two things | To run very fast | A picture you click on a website
exercise: «The chain had one broken link.» What does link mean here? | A word you click | To join two things | *A ring or loop of a chain | A connection between ideas
exercise: «Click the link to go to the next page.» What does link mean here? | A connection between two people | *A word or picture you click to go to another page | To join arms | A ring of a chain
test: «Please link arms when you cross the road.» What does link mean here? | A word you click on a website | Something you break | A ring in a chain | *To join or connect
test: «The teacher asked us to link the two ideas.» What does link mean here? | *To connect or join | A loop of metal | To break apart | Something you click
exam: «Think before you click a link in a strange message, and ask an adult if you are unsure.» What does the sentence warn you about? | Links only appear in chains | Links are always safe, so click them all | Strange messages are always funny | *Links in strange messages can lead to harmful sites or tricks
